# Landing page
You can pass in multiple files into `squid`.
If you pass in more than 1 file, the `--output` flag
is required. `squid` can output both `zip` and `tar` gunzip archives of your queries.

```bash
cd ./examples/multi-page # from repo root

squid ./index.squid ./users.squid ./benchmarks.squid \
    -o ./content.zip
```

To specify HTML as the output file format, use the
`.html.{COMPRESSION_NAME}` file extension to tell `squid` to use
HTML instead of plain markdown:

```bash
cd ./examples/multi-page # from repo root

squid ./index.squid ./users.squid ./benchmarks.squid \
    -o ./content.html.zip

squid ./index.squid ./users.squid ./benchmarks.squid \
    -o ./content.html.tar.gz
```

You can also specify a glob pattern instead of
individually listing out each file:

```bash
squid ./examples/multi-page/*.squid # from repo root (make this the glob for just at this directory if my glob is wrong)
squid ./examples/multi-page/**/*.squid # from repo root (make this the recursive glob if my glob is wrong and retains input-dir structure in output)
```

So we should be able to see this:

{SELECT username FROM users LIMIT 1}

See the other pages:

- [users](./users.squid)
- [benchmarks](./benchmarks.squid)
